{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 4380,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are tiny molecular machines, and they are doing this inside your body - right now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11040,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand why, we have to zoom out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15240,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every day, in an adult human body, 50 to 70 billion of your cells die."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20800,
    "dDurationMs": 7060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Either they're stressed, or damaged, or just old. But this is normal - in fact, it's called \"programmed cell death\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28100,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, to make up for all these lost cells, right now, billions of your cells are dividing,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33680,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "essentially creating new cells. And that process of cell division, also called mitosis --"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38520,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well, it requires an army of tiny molecular machines. So, let's take a closer look."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45420,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "DNA is a good place to start - the double helix molecule we always talk about."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49420,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a scientifically accurate depiction of DNA, created by Drew Berry"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53540,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the Walter and Eliza Hall Institute of Medical Research."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56960,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you unwind the two strands, you can see that each has a sugar-phosphate backbone"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61180,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "connected to the sequence of nucleic acid base pairs, known by the letters A, T, G and C."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66920,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, the strands run in opposite directions, which is important when you go to copy DNA."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71960,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Copying DNA is one of the first steps in cell division."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77980,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here,  the two strands of DNA are being unwound and separated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81540,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the tiny blue molecular machine called \"helicase\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85080,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Helicase literally spins as fast as a jet engine!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89740,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The strand of DNA on the right has its complementary strand assembled continuously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94300,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the other strand is more complicated, because it runs in the opposite direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98620,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it must be looped out with its complementary strand assembled in reverse, section by section."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105580,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the end of this process, you have two identical DNA molecules, each one a few centimeters long,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111620,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but just a couple nanometers wide."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114280,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, to prevent the DNA from becoming a tangled mess, it is wrapped around proteins called \"histones\","
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120180,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forming a nucleosome."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122080,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These nucleosomes are bundled together into a fiber known as chromatin, which is further looped and coiled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127820,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to form a chromosome, one of the largest molecular structures in your body."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152440,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can actually see chromosomes under a microscope in dividing cells."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156260,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only then do they take on their characteristic shape. Otherwise, the DNA is more strewn inside the nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165620,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The process of dividing a cell takes around an hour in mammals, so this footage is from a time-lapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171280,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can see how the chromosomes line up on the equator of the cell."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178400,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, when everything is right, they are pulled apart into the two new daughter cells,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183580,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each one containing an identical copy of DNA."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188220,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, simple as this looks, the process is incredibly complicated and requires"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192420,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even more fascinating molecular machines to accomplish it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196060,
    "dDurationMs": 2540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, let's look at a single chromosome."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198860,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One chromosome consists of two sausage shaped chromatids, containing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203020,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the identical copies of DNA made earlier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205760,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each chromatid is attached to microtubule fibers, which guide and help align them in the correct position."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211700,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The microtubules are connected to the chromatid at the kinetochore, here colored red."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216600,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The kinetochore consists of hundreds of different proteins working together to achieve multiple"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221660,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objectives. In fact, it's one of the most sophisticated molecular mechanisms inside your body."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The kinetochore is central to the successful separation of the chromatids. It creates a dynamic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232920,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "connection between the chromosome and the microtubules."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236320,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a reason no one's yet been able to figure out, the microtubules are constantly being built at one end"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241380,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and deconstructed at the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243880,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the chromosome is still getting ready, the kinetochore sends out a chemical \"stop\" signal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248500,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the rest of the cell, shown here by the red molecules,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251440,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "basically saying, \"this chromosome is not yet ready to divide.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260000,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The kinetochore also mechanically senses tension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263880,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the tension is just right, and the position and attachment are correct,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267980,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all the proteins get ready, shown here by turning green."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271440,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this point, the stop signal broadcasting system is not switched off!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275760,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, it is literally carried away from the kinetochore, down the microtubules,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280500,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by a dynein motor - that's the walking guy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283760,
    "dDurationMs": 7180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is really what it looks like: it has long \"legs\" so it can avoid obstacles and step over the kinesins,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290940,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "molecular motors that walk in the opposite direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295760,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Personally, I'm astounded by these tiny molecular machines, how they're able to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300280,
    "dDurationMs": 7060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "routinely and faithfully execute their functions billions of times over inside your body at this exact instant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'm also amazed by the scientists who were able to work out how this happens"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312240,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in such detail that we could create realistic depictions of them, like you saw in the animations in this video."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318620,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, perhaps, the most amazing thing is just how much is left to be discovered, like,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323180,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "figuring out how exactly the chromatids are pulled to opposite ends of the cell."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327520,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is still so much that we don't quite know."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330900,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You know, what I find exciting is, that in science fiction, for decades,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334320,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we've been writing about tiny nanobots that will be injected into our blood streams that can heal us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339920,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, what this suggests, the existence of these tiny molecular machines inside us,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344300,
    "dDurationMs": 5789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it suggests that there isn't a physical limit that would prevent that. And so, I think it's pretty likely that, in future,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350089,
    "dDurationMs": 3791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we will be able to develop our own tiny molecular machines"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353880,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that will be able to repair our bodies better than they can repair themselves."
    } ]
  } ]
}
